        "en": {
          "why_it_matters": "The event is the cycle, not a star recital with familiar repertoire. Beethoven's ten violin sonatas trace an enormous change in what violin and piano can demand from one another, and Vengerov and Argerich are taking that whole arc across three consecutive Carnegie nights. Argerich's first return to the Hall in a decade adds real historical weight, but the stronger musical reason is the sustained duo conversation: three evenings in which two highly individual players have to keep renegotiating balance, attack, freedom and structure across an entire body of work rather than one showcase sonata.",
